W=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;B=W.parent/'ophthalmic-work/inr-anatomy-atlas-v0.9.120';m=json.loads((B/'anatomy/generated/complete_manifest.json').read_text());byid={s['id']:s for s in m['structures']};changes={r['id']:r for r in json.loads((W/'changes-partial.json').read_text())}
brain={
0:'The wing of the central lobule is the lateral hemispheric continuation of the central lobule of the anterior cerebellum.',
1:'The uvula is a lobule of the inferior vermis, situated between the pyramis posteriorly and the nodule anteriorly, near the cerebellar tonsils.',
2:'The tuber is a lobular region of the posterior vermis, between the folium and pyramis.',
3:'The cerebellar tonsil is a rounded lobule on the inferomedial cerebellar hemisphere, beside the medulla and near the foramen magnum.',
4:'The superior semilunar lobule occupies the upper posterior cerebellar hemisphere, above the horizontal fissure. It corresponds broadly to crus I in commonly used lobular nomenclature.',
5:'The superior cerebellar peduncle connects the cerebellum with the midbrain and rostral brainstem. It lies beside the upper fourth ventricle and carries predominantly cerebellar output fibres.',
6:'The pyramis is a lobule of the inferior vermis between the tuber and uvula.',
7:'The posterior quadrangular lobule lies on the superior cerebellar hemisphere behind the primary fissure and is continuous medially with the declive.',
8:'The peduncle of the flocculus is the narrow attachment of the flocculus to the cerebellum near the lateral recess of the fourth ventricle.',
9:'The nodule is the most anterior lobule of the inferior vermis and contributes to the inferior aspect of the fourth ventricular roof. It forms the flocculonodular lobe with the paired flocculi.',
10:'The lingula is a small anterior-superior vermian lobule closely related to the superior medullary velum.',
11:'The inferior semilunar lobule lies below the horizontal fissure on the posterior cerebellar hemisphere. It corresponds broadly to crus II in commonly used lobular nomenclature.',
12:'The gracile lobule is part of the inferior-posterior cerebellar hemisphere, below the semilunar region and above the biventral lobule.',
13:'The folium is a thin posterior vermian lobule between the declive and tuber, near the posterior end of the horizontal fissure.',
14:'The flocculus is a small lobule on the anteroinferior cerebellar surface near the middle cerebellar peduncle and lateral recess of the fourth ventricle.',
15:'The declive is the superior vermian region immediately posterior to the primary fissure and culmen.',
16:'The culmen is the prominent superior vermian region of the anterior cerebellar lobe, anterior to the primary fissure.',
17:'The central lobule is an anterior vermian lobule between the lingula and culmen. It extends laterally into the wings of the central lobule.',
18:'The biventral lobule lies on the inferior cerebellar hemisphere, lateral to the tonsil and below the gracile region.',
19:'The anterior quadrangular lobule occupies the superior cerebellar hemisphere anterior to the primary fissure and is continuous medially with the culmen.',
20:'The pons is the brainstem segment between the midbrain and medulla. Its ventral surface faces the prepontine cistern and basilar artery, while its dorsal surface contributes to the floor of the fourth ventricle.',
21:'The fourth ventricle is the CSF space between the dorsal pons and upper medulla anteriorly and the cerebellar roof structures posteriorly. It communicates with the cerebral aqueduct superiorly and subarachnoid spaces through its median and lateral apertures.',
22:'The medulla is the caudal brainstem between the pons and spinal cord. Its ventral surface bears the pyramids and olives; its upper dorsal surface contributes to the floor of the fourth ventricle.',
23:'The tentorium cerebelli is a dural fold separating the occipital cerebral hemispheres from the cerebellum. Its free medial edge bounds the tentorial incisura; its junction with the falx contains the straight sinus.',
24:'The falx cerebri is the midline dural fold between the cerebral hemispheres. The superior sagittal sinus lies in its attached superior margin, the inferior sagittal sinus in its free inferior margin, and the straight sinus along its junction with the tentorium.',
25:'The lateral ventricle is a C-shaped CSF cavity within a cerebral hemisphere, with frontal, temporal and occipital horns around a central body and atrium. It communicates with the third ventricle through the interventricular foramen.',
26:'The thalamus is a paired diencephalic structure bordering the third ventricle medially and the internal capsule laterally. Its superior surface lies beneath the body of the lateral ventricle.',
27:'The third ventricle is a midline CSF cavity between the thalami and hypothalamic regions. It communicates with the lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina and with the fourth ventricle through the cerebral aqueduct.',
28:'The midbrain lies between the diencephalon and pons. Its ventral surface contains the cerebral peduncles and interpeduncular region, while the colliculi form its dorsal tectum around the cerebral aqueduct.',
29:'The pars opercularis is the posterior part of the inferior frontal gyrus, behind the anterior ascending ramus of the lateral fissure and anterior to the inferior precentral region.',
30:'The pars orbitalis is the ventral part of the inferior frontal gyrus, below the anterior horizontal ramus of the lateral fissure and adjoining the orbital frontal surface.',
31:'The pars triangularis is the portion of the inferior frontal gyrus between the anterior ascending and anterior horizontal rami of the lateral fissure.',
32:'The frontomarginal gyral and sulcal region lies near the frontal pole. This combined source-atlas label should not be interpreted as the complete orbital frontal cortex.',
33:'The paracentral region surrounds the medial end of the central sulcus and includes medial continuations of the precentral and postcentral gyri. This atlas label combines gyral and sulcal references.',
34:'The transverse frontopolar gyral and sulcal region lies at the anterior end of the frontal lobe. Its small folds and boundaries vary between individuals.',
35:'The gyrus rectus lies on the medial orbital surface of the frontal lobe, medial to the olfactory sulcus.',
36:'The anterior ascending ramus of the lateral fissure runs superiorly into the inferior frontal gyrus, separating the pars triangularis from the pars opercularis.',
37:'The inferior frontal sulcus separates the middle frontal gyrus above from the inferior frontal gyrus below.',
38:'The middle frontal gyrus lies between the superior and inferior frontal sulci, anterior to the precentral region.',
39:'The orbital gyri occupy the inferior frontal surface lateral to the olfactory sulcus. The orbital sulci divide this surface into variable anterior, posterior, medial and lateral regions.',
40:'The precentral gyrus lies immediately anterior to the central sulcus and contains the primary motor cortex. It extends medially into the anterior paracentral region.',
41:'The superior frontal gyrus occupies the upper frontal surface and continues onto the medial hemisphere. The superior frontal sulcus forms its lateral boundary.',
42:'The superior frontal sulcus separates the superior and middle frontal gyri and usually runs anteriorly from the precentral region.',
43:'The paracentral sulcus lies on the medial frontal surface and helps define the anterior border of the paracentral lobule.',
44:'The H-shaped orbital sulci divide the orbital frontal surface into medial, lateral, anterior and posterior orbital gyral regions. Their configuration is variable.',
45:'The lateral orbital sulcus is part of the variable orbital sulcal pattern on the inferior frontal surface, separating adjacent orbital gyral regions.',
46:'The olfactory sulcus lies on the orbital frontal surface between the gyrus rectus medially and medial orbital gyrus laterally. It accommodates the olfactory bulb and tract.',
47:'The inferior part of the precentral sulcus lies anterior to the lower precentral gyrus, near the inferior frontal and opercular regions.',
48:'The superior part of the precentral sulcus lies anterior to the upper precentral gyrus and commonly approaches the superior frontal sulcus.',
49:'The anterior horizontal ramus of the lateral fissure extends anteriorly into the inferior frontal gyrus and separates its orbital and triangular parts.',
50:'The angular gyrus forms the posterior part of the inferior parietal lobule and curves around the posterior end of the superior temporal sulcus.',
51:'The supramarginal gyrus forms the anterior part of the inferior parietal lobule and curves around the posterior end of the lateral fissure.',
52:'The postcentral gyrus lies immediately posterior to the central sulcus and contains the primary somatosensory cortex. Its medial continuation enters the posterior paracentral region.',
53:'The postcentral sulcus lies behind the postcentral gyrus and separates it from more posterior parietal cortex.',
54:'The precuneus lies on the medial parietal surface, between the marginal cingulate sulcus anteriorly, parieto-occipital sulcus posteriorly and subparietal region inferiorly.',
55:'The superior parietal lobule lies behind the postcentral region and above the intraparietal sulcus. It continues medially towards the precuneus.',
56:'The intraparietal sulcus separates the superior and inferior parietal lobules and runs posteriorly from the postcentral region towards the occipital lobe.',
57:'The occipital pole is the posterior extremity of the cerebral hemisphere, where medial, lateral and inferior occipital surfaces converge.',
58:'This combined inferior occipital gyral and sulcal parcel lies on the lower lateral or inferolateral occipital surface. The local gyral pattern is variable.',
59:'The lingual gyrus lies on the inferomedial occipital surface between the calcarine sulcus above and collateral sulcus below.',
60:'The middle or lateral occipital gyral region occupies the lateral occipital surface between superior and inferior occipital regions. Its sulcal boundaries are variable.',
61:'The calcarine sulcus lies on the medial occipital surface between the cuneus and lingual gyrus. Primary visual cortex is arranged along its banks.',
62:'The cuneus is the medial occipital region between the parieto-occipital sulcus and the superior bank of the calcarine sulcus.',
63:'The superior occipital gyri occupy the upper occipital surface posterior to the parietal region. Their individual folds and boundaries vary.',
64:'The lunate sulcus is a variable groove on the lateral occipital surface. Its identification and relationship to neighbouring occipital folds are inconsistent between individuals.',
65:'The transverse occipital sulcus runs across the posterior superior or lateral occipital surface and may be continuous with the posterior intraparietal sulcus.',
66:'The temporal pole is the anterior extremity of the temporal lobe, where the superior, lateral and inferior temporal surfaces converge.',
67:'The transverse temporal gyri, including Heschl\'s gyrus, lie on the superior temporal plane within the lateral fissure and contain primary auditory cortical regions.',
68:'The temporal plane is the superior surface of the temporal lobe buried within the lateral fissure, around the transverse temporal gyri and adjacent planar cortex.',
69:'The lateral part of the superior temporal gyrus lies below the lateral fissure and above the superior temporal sulcus.',
70:'The lateral occipitotemporal gyral region lies on the ventral temporal-occipital surface beside the occipitotemporal sulcus.',
71:'This medial occipitotemporal parcel includes the parahippocampal region on the medial ventral temporal surface. It lies beside the collateral sulcus and hippocampal formation.',
72:'The inferior temporal sulcus separates the middle and inferior temporal gyri on the lateral temporal surface.',
73:'The inferior temporal gyrus lies below the inferior temporal sulcus and extends towards the ventral temporal surface.',
74:'The middle temporal gyrus lies between the superior and inferior temporal sulci on the lateral temporal surface.',
75:'The superior temporal sulcus separates the superior and middle temporal gyri. Its posterior branches extend towards the inferior parietal region.',
76:'The occipitotemporal sulcus separates adjacent lateral and medial portions of the ventral temporal-occipital gyral surface.',
77:'The subcentral gyrus bridges the lower ends of the precentral and postcentral gyri around the inferior end of the central sulcus. This combined source-atlas label includes adjoining opercular sulcal references and should not be read as the boundary of the whole insula.',
78:'The marginal branch of the cingulate sulcus ascends on the medial hemisphere behind the paracentral lobule and separates it from the precuneus.',
79:'This anterior-middle cingulate parcel lies on the medial hemisphere above the corpus callosum, with the cingulate sulcus forming its superior boundary.',
80:'This posterior-middle cingulate parcel lies along the medial hemisphere above the posterior callosal body, between anterior cingulate and posterior cingulate regions.',
81:'The dorsal posterior cingulate region lies above the posterior corpus callosum and continues towards the precuneus and subparietal region.',
82:'The posteroventral cingulate region curves around the splenium of the corpus callosum towards the retrosplenial and parahippocampal regions.',
83:'The hippocampus is a curved medial temporal structure forming part of the floor of the temporal horn of the lateral ventricle. It is continuous with the fimbria-fornix pathway.',
84:'The crus cerebri forms the ventral part of the cerebral peduncle in the midbrain and contains descending cortical fibres. It lies anterior to the substantia nigra.',
85:'The cerebral aqueduct is the narrow CSF passage through the midbrain linking the third and fourth ventricles. The tectum lies dorsally and the tegmentum ventrally.',
86:'The superior colliculus forms the upper paired elevation of the dorsal midbrain tectum, below the pineal region and above the inferior colliculus.',
87:'The inferior colliculus forms the lower paired elevation of the dorsal midbrain tectum, below the superior colliculus and above the superior medullary velum.',
88:'The corpus callosum is the major commissural white-matter connection between the cerebral hemispheres. Its rostrum, genu, body and splenium lie beneath the pericallosal cistern.',
89:'The hypothalamus forms the ventral part of the diencephalon, below the thalamus and around the lower third ventricle. It includes the infundibular and mammillary regions.',
90:'The septum pellucidum is a thin paired membrane between the frontal horns and bodies of the lateral ventricles, extending between the corpus callosum and fornix.',
91:'The choroid plexus is vascular tissue within the ventricular system that contributes to CSF production. The lateral ventricular plexus follows the choroidal fissure along the body, atrium and temporal horn.',
92:'The caudate nucleus curves alongside the lateral ventricle, with its head beside the frontal horn, body along the ventricular body and tail following the temporal horn.',
93:'The fornix is a white-matter pathway associated with the hippocampal formation. Its crura and body curve beneath the corpus callosum, and its columns descend near the interventricular foramina.',
94:'The optic tract continues posteriorly from the optic chiasm around the cerebral peduncle towards the lateral geniculate region. It is distinct from the prechiasmatic optic nerve.',
95:'The optic chiasm lies above the pituitary region and anterior to the infundibulum, where fibres from the optic nerves partially cross before continuing in the optic tracts.',
96:'The central sulcus separates the precentral and postcentral gyri and forms a major boundary between the frontal and parietal lobes. It extends from the superomedial margin towards the lateral fissure.',
97:'The parieto-occipital sulcus separates the precuneus from the cuneus on the medial hemisphere and joins the calcarine sulcal region inferiorly.',
98:'The circular or peri-insular sulcus surrounds most of the insula and separates it from the frontal, parietal and temporal opercula. It is interrupted anteroinferiorly near the limen insulae.',
99:'The posterior ramus of the lateral fissure separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal opercula above. The insula lies deep to these opercula.',
100:'The globus pallidus is the medial component of the lentiform nucleus, lying medial to the putamen and lateral to the internal capsule.',
101:'The putamen is the lateral component of the lentiform nucleus, lying lateral to the globus pallidus and medial to the external capsule.',
193:'The upper cervical spinal cord continues inferiorly from the medulla near the foramen magnum. This illustrative surface provides regional context for spinal vessels and does not resolve individual nerve roots or vertebral levels.',
194:'The anterior petroclinoid fold is a dural fold between the petrous apex region and anterior clinoid process, near the lateral cavernous sinus roof. Its detailed dural relationships vary.',
195:'The posterior petroclinoid fold extends between the petrous apex region and posterior clinoid process near the posterior cavernous region and tentorial attachment.'}
