bone={
0:'The inferior orbital fissure is the gap between the orbital floor and lateral wall, communicating with the pterygopalatine and infratemporal regions. It transmits infraorbital and zygomatic neurovascular structures and orbital venous communications.',
1:'The infraorbital groove runs forward on the orbital floor and continues as the infraorbital canal within the maxilla. It carries the infraorbital nerve, artery and vein to the infraorbital foramen.',
2:'The infraorbital foramen opens on the anterior maxilla below the orbital margin. It transmits the infraorbital nerve, artery and vein to the face.',
3:'A variable cranio-orbital or meningolacrimal canal in the sphenoid region connects the middle cranial fossa with the orbit. It may transmit a meningolacrimal artery; its presence and relationship to other orbital openings vary.',
4:'The foramen rotundum is a sphenoid canal connecting the middle cranial fossa with the pterygopalatine fossa. It transmits the maxillary nerve (V2) and small accompanying vessels.',
5:'The foramen ovale opens between the middle cranial fossa and infratemporal region in the greater sphenoid wing. It transmits the mandibular nerve (V3), emissary venous channels and variable small vessels including an accessory meningeal contribution.',
6:'The foramen spinosum is an opening in the greater sphenoid wing, posterolateral to the foramen ovale. It usually transmits the middle meningeal artery, accompanying veins and the recurrent meningeal branch of V3.',
7:'The foramen lacerum lies at the junction of the sphenoid, petrous temporal and basilar occipital regions and is largely filled by cartilage in life. The internal carotid artery passes over its superior aspect rather than through an open vertical lumen. Petrosal neural and small vascular connections relate to this region.',
8:'The sphenoidal emissary foramen of Vesalius is a variable opening near the foramen ovale. It commonly carries an emissary venous connection between the cavernous region and pterygoid plexus; small arterial contributions are variable.',
9:'The carotid canal traverses the petrous temporal bone from the skull base towards the foramen lacerum region. It contains the petrous internal carotid artery, sympathetic fibres and a venous plexus.',
10:'The stylomastoid foramen opens between the styloid and mastoid processes and marks the extracranial exit of the facial nerve. The stylomastoid artery accompanies the nerve into the facial canal.',
11:'The petrotympanic fissure communicates between the tympanic region and the mandibular fossa region. Structures associated with it include the chorda tympani and anterior tympanic vessels.',
12:'The tympanic canaliculus opens near the jugular fossa and carotid canal and ascends to the middle ear. It carries the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve and the inferior tympanic artery.',
13:'The jugular foramen lies between the temporal and occipital bones and transmits cranial nerves IX, X and XI with the sigmoid-jugular and inferior petrosal venous drainage pathways. The jugular fossa accommodates the jugular bulb.',
14:'The hypoglossal canal traverses the occipital bone above the occipital condyle. It transmits the hypoglossal nerve, a venous plexus and small meningeal arterial branches.',
15:'The mastoid foramen is a variable opening in or near the occipitomastoid region. It transmits the mastoid emissary vein and often a meningeal branch of the occipital artery.',
16:'The foramen magnum is the large occipital opening between the posterior cranial fossa and vertebral canal. It transmits the medulla-spinal cord transition, meninges, vertebral and spinal vessels and ascending spinal accessory roots. Vertebral dural entry is distinct from passage through the posterior atlanto-occipital membrane.',
17:'The sphenopalatine foramen connects the pterygopalatine fossa with the nasal cavity at the superior palatine-sphenoid region. It transmits sphenopalatine vessels and associated nasal neural branches.',
18:'The pterygoid or vidian canal runs through the base of the pterygoid process between the foramen lacerum region and pterygopalatine fossa. It contains the nerve and vessels of the pterygoid canal.',
19:'The palatovaginal or pharyngeal canal communicates between the pterygopalatine fossa and roof of the nasopharynx. It carries the pharyngeal branch of the maxillary artery and an accompanying pharyngeal nerve.',
20:'The greater palatine canal descends from the pterygopalatine fossa towards the palate, between the maxillary and palatine regions. It carries the descending palatine vessels and palatine nerves.',
21:'The greater palatine foramen opens on the posterolateral hard palate. It transmits the greater palatine neurovascular bundle, which then runs anteriorly along the palate.',
22:'The mandibular canal runs within the mandible from the mandibular foramen on the medial ramus towards the mental and incisive pathways. It contains the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels.',
23:'The vertebral artery groove lies on the superior surface of the posterior arch of the atlas. The extradural vertebral artery crosses it before turning towards its dural entry.',
24:'An arcuate foramen is a variable bony bridge over the vertebral artery groove of the atlas. When present, it partly or completely roofs the regional vertebral neurovascular corridor.',
25:'The petrolingual ligament forms a regional boundary between the petrous and cavernous ICA in the NYU classification. It extends near the petrous apex and sphenoid lingula; its position is estimated in this atlas.',
26:'The anterior clinoid process is the medial projection of the lesser sphenoid wing beside the optic canal and clinoid ICA. It relates to the optic strut, proximal and distal dural rings and the carotid-oculomotor region.',
27:'The proximal and distal dural rings bound the short clinoid ICA region. The distal ring marks the usual transition to the intradural ICA, with a variable carotid cave medially. This atlas marks a regional estimate rather than separately segmented dural rings.'}
